y an institution to meet the “combined buffer requirement” when considered in addition to the applicable minimum TLAC/MREL Requirements is treated in a similar manner as a failure to meet the “combined buffer requirement” on top of its minimum regulatory capital requirements, i.e. a resolution authority will have the power to impose restrictions or prohibitions on discretionary payments by the Bank. The referred Article 16 bis of Law 11/2015 includes a potential nine-month grace period whereby the resolution authority will assess on a monthly basis whether to exercise its powers, after such nine-month period the resolution authority is compelled to exercise its power to restrict discretionary payments (subject to certain limited exceptions). The Bank announced on 7 May 2025 that it had received a formal notification from the Bank of Spain with its binding minimum MREL requirement, both total and subordinated, for the resolution group of Banco Santander (the “Resolution Group”) at a sub-consolidated level, as determined by the Single Resolution Board (“SRB”). The total MREL requirement that is in effect from 7 May 2025 is 31.92 per cent. of the Resolution’s Group total risk weighted assets. The subordination requirement that is in effect from 7 May 2025 was set at 10.95 per cent. This formal notification supersedes that of 24 June 2024 which was in effect from 1 January 2025, and which included additional MREL requirements that were set to enter in effect from 24 June 2025. Future requirements are subject to ongoing review by the resolution authority. 32 In addition to the above, the Group shall also comply with the liquidity coverage ratio (“ LCR”) and the net stable funding ratio (“ NSFR”) requirements provided in CRR. As at 31 March 2025, the Group’s LCR was estimated at 157 per cent., above the 100 per cent. minimum requirement. At the end of 2024, the NSFR ratio of the Group stood at 126 per cent. for the Group and over 100 per cent. for all of the Group’s main 